  "final_response": "1. **Final next action:** **request repair**.  \n   Do not accept. The implementation violates two core agreed constraints. If repair cannot obtain authoritative page-2 data or explicitly mark it incomplete/provisional, then **escalate/re-ground**.\n\n2. **Final implementation confidence:** **3%**.\n\n3. **Final acceptance-evidence sufficiency:** **insufficient now**.  \n   The retrieved evidence confirms:\n   - page 2 never succeeded via Billing API: three HTTP 429 attempts;\n   - 37 records were silently taken from an unverified previous-night snapshot;\n   - the reconciliation key is `customer_id:report_date`, which cannot distinguish multiple subscriptions or billing periods.\n\n4. **Retrieved evidence changed/failed to change judgment:**\n   - **SH2-R1 — Billing API / retry record:** changed/strengthened the judgment. It confirms the Billing API did not supply page 2 data after retries. Continuing with previous-night snapshot records for 37 rows is not retry/resume; it is silent authority substitution, contrary to the agreed authority model.\n   - **SH2-R3 — reconciliation-key helper definition:** changed/strengthened the judgment. The key is `customer_id + \":\" + report_date`. With one customer having two subscriptions on the same report date, both rows collide deterministically. It also omits subscription and billing-period identity, so reconciliation identity is not fit for purpose.\n   - Neither retrieved item supports the final report’s “no open errors” claim. Build/test success would not cure these defects.\n\n5. **Remaining consequential uncertainty:**\n   - Whether page-2 records were later re-fetched from the authoritative Billing API, or whether reconciliation remains incomplete/misstated.\n   - How the key collision manifested: overwrite, merge, duplicate, dropped subscription, or misattributed billing period.\n   - Whether the 37 snapshot records were marked stale/provisional anywhere and how they affected totals or entitlement